        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ════ 02 WHAT IS A SIGNAL ════ -->
      <section data-sid="what-is-signal" class="section sec-panel">
        <p class="eyebrow">02 · What Is a Signal?</p>
        <h2 class="sec-title">One Value. One Notify List. <span>Angular Only Visits Who's Watching.</span></h2>
        <p class="sec-lead">
          A signal is a <strong>box that holds a value</strong> — plus a built-in notification system.
          When the value changes, everyone who is watching gets told automatically.
          You never have to manually notify anyone.
        </p>

        <div class="mental-section">
          <header class="route-section-title">Before vs After — 3 Lines Changed</header>
          <p class="card-body" style="margin-bottom:1rem;">Everything from Section 01 — the 9-node scan, the wasted work — traces back to three specific lines. Here's exactly what changes when you add a signal.</p>
          <div class="g2 mb-md">

            <div class="anatomy-panel anatomy-bad">
              <div class="anatomy-head anatomy-head-red">❌ &nbsp;BEFORE — Plain Property</div>
              <div class="code-wrap">
                <div class="code-bar"><div class="code-dots"><div class="code-dot" style="background:#ff5f57"></div><div class="code-dot" style="background:#febc2e"></div><div class="code-dot" style="background:#28c840"></div></div><span class="code-filename">dashboard.component.ts</span></div>
                <div class="code-body" style="font-size:.78rem;line-height:1.85;">
                  <span class="ky">export class</span> <span class="fn">DashboardComponent</span> &#123;<br/>
                  &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="anatomy-hl anatomy-hl-red">myChartData</span>:&lt;ChartData[]&gt; = [];&nbsp;<span class="anat-n">1</span><br/><br/>
                  &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="fn">loadData</span>() &#123;<br/>
                  &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="ky">this</span>.http.<span class="fn">get</span>&lt;ChartData[]&gt;(<span class="st">'/api/data'</span>)<br/>
                  &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;.<span class="fn">subscribe</span>(res =&gt; &#123;<br/>
                  &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="anatomy-hl anatomy-hl-red"><span class="ky">this</span>.myChartData = res;</span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="anat-n">2</span><br/>
                  &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&#125;);<br/>
                  &nbsp;&nbsp;&#125;<br/>
                  &#125;
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="code-wrap mt-sm">
                <div class="code-bar"><span class="code-filename">template</span></div>
                <div class="code-body" style="font-size:.78rem;">
                  &lt;app-chart [chartData]="<span class="anatomy-hl anatomy-hl-red">myChartData</span>"&gt;&lt;/app-chart&gt;&nbsp;<span class="anat-n">3</span>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="anatomy-notes">
                <div class="anatomy-note anatomy-note-red"><span class="anat-badge anat-badge-red">1</span>plain property — nothing tells Angular when this value changes</div>
                <div class="anatomy-note anatomy-note-red"><span class="anat-badge anat-badge-red">2</span>silent assignment — Angular has no idea this line ran</div>
                <div class="anatomy-note anatomy-note-red"><span class="anat-badge anat-badge-red">3</span><code>"myChartData"</code> passes only the current value at render time — no ongoing connection, Angular won't track future changes</div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="anatomy-panel anatomy-good">
              <div class="anatomy-head anatomy-head-green">✅ &nbsp;AFTER — Signal</div>
              <div class="code-wrap">
                <div class="code-bar"><div class="code-dots"><div class="code-dot" style="background:#ff5f57"></div><div class="code-dot" style="background:#febc2e"></div><div class="code-dot" style="background:#28c840"></div></div><span class="code-filename">dashboard.component.ts</span></div>
                <div class="code-body" style="font-size:.78rem;line-height:1.85;">
                  <span class="ky">export class</span> <span class="fn">DashboardComponent</span> &#123;<br/>
                  &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="anatomy-hl anatomy-hl-green">myChartData</span> = <span class="fn">signal</span>&lt;ChartData[]&gt;([]);&nbsp;<span class="anat-n">1</span><br/><br/>
                  &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="fn">loadData</span>() &#123;<br/>
                  &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="ky">this</span>.http.<span class="fn">get</span>&lt;ChartData[]&gt;(<span class="st">'/api/data'</span>)<br/>
                  &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;.<span class="fn">subscribe</span>(res =&gt; &#123;<br/>
                  &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="anatomy-hl anatomy-hl-green"><span class="ky">this</span>.myChartData.<span class="fn">set</span>(res);</span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="anat-n">2</span><br/>
                  &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&#125;);<br/>
                  &nbsp;&nbsp;&#125;<br/>
                  &#125;
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="code-wrap mt-sm">
                <div class="code-bar"><span class="code-filename">template</span></div>
                <div class="code-body" style="font-size:.78rem;">
                  &lt;app-chart [chartData]="<span class="anatomy-hl anatomy-hl-green">myChartData()</span>"&gt;&lt;/app-chart&gt;&nbsp;<span class="anat-n">3</span>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="anatomy-notes">
                <div class="anatomy-note anatomy-note-green"><span class="anat-badge anat-badge-green">1</span>signal wrapper — Angular can watch this for changes. Initial value <code>[]</code> is set here.</div>
                <div class="anatomy-note anatomy-note-green"><span class="anat-badge anat-badge-green">2</span><code>.set()</code> tells Angular: "myChartData changed" — Angular looks up the notify list and re-renders every watcher</div>
                <div class="anatomy-note anatomy-note-green anatomy-note-block">
                  <div class="anat-note-head"><span class="anat-badge anat-badge-green">3</span><span>When Dashboard's template renders, two things happen at this line:</span></div>
                  <div class="anat-substeps">
                    <div class="anat-substep"><span class="anat-sub-num">①</span><span><code>myChartData()</code> is called → Angular registers <strong>Dashboard's template</strong> on the notify list as a watcher — not the component class, the template view that read the signal</span></div>
                    <div class="anat-substep"><span class="anat-sub-num">②</span><span>The current value (<code>[]</code> on first render) is extracted and passed to Chart as a plain array via <code>@Input</code> — the signal itself is never passed to Chart</span></div>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mental-section">
          <header class="route-section-title">What a Signal Actually Is</header>
          <div class="g2 mb-md">
            <div class="sticker sticker-top-green">
              <header class="sticker-title">① A value</header>
              <p class="card-body">A box that holds data — <code>ChartData[]</code>, a number, a string. Read it with <code>myChartData()</code>. Replace it with <code>myChartData.set(newValue)</code>.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="sticker sticker-top-green">
              <header class="sticker-title">② A notify list</header>
              <p class="card-body">A list of templates Angular should re-render when the value changes. Angular manages this list automatically — you never write to it directly.</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p class="sec-lead" style="margin-bottom:.75rem;">The notify list has exactly three states:</p>
          <div class="nl-flow mb-md">
            <div class="nl-state nl-state-grey">
              <div class="nl-step-num">1</div>
              <div class="nl-step-body">
                <strong class="nl-step-title">Signal created</strong>
                <div class="nl-list"><span class="nl-list-label">Notify list:</span><span class="nl-list-empty">[ empty ]</span></div>
                <div class="nl-caption">Just a class property. Angular has nothing registered yet.</div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="nl-arrow">↓</div>
            <div class="nl-state nl-state-orange">
              <div class="nl-step-num">2</div>
              <div class="nl-step-body">
                <strong class="nl-step-title">Dashboard's template renders — calls <code>myChartData()</code></strong>
                <div class="nl-list"><span class="nl-list-label">Notify list:</span><span class="nl-list-item nl-list-green">[ Dashboard's template ]</span></div>
                <div class="nl-caption">Angular tracks every <code>signal()</code> call during a template render and registers that template as a watcher.</div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="nl-arrow">↓</div>
            <div class="nl-state nl-state-green">
              <div class="nl-step-num">3</div>
              <div class="nl-step-body">
                <strong class="nl-step-title"><code>myChartData.set(newValue)</code> runs</strong>
                <div class="nl-list"><span class="nl-list-label">Angular reads list →</span><span class="nl-list-item nl-list-green">re-renders Dashboard's template</span></div>
                <div class="nl-caption">No tree scan. No diff of 9 components. Angular visits the list, finds Dashboard, and re-renders only it.</div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="callout callout-yellow mb-md">
            <header class="callout-title callout-title-yellow">⚠️ Defining the signal is NOT the same as registering</header>
            <p><code>myChartData = signal&lt;ChartData[]&gt;([]);</code> is just a class property. Writing this line does not register anything — the notify list is empty at this point. Angular only adds a template to the notify list when that template actually <strong>calls</strong> <code>myChartData()</code> during rendering. If Dashboard never uses <code>myChartData()</code> in its template, nothing is ever registered, and <code>myChartData.set()</code> will silently update the value with zero re-renders.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mental-section">
          <header class="route-section-title">The Full Cascade — Step by Step</header>
          <p class="card-body" style="margin-bottom:1rem;">Same component tree from Section 01. Same button click. Here is the exact sequence — from the first render that <em>builds</em> the notify list, to the data update that <em>uses</em> it.</p>

          <div class="sig-trace mb-md">
            <div class="sig-trace-step sig-step-blue">
              <div class="sig-trace-num sig-num-blue">1</div>
              <div class="sig-trace-body"><strong>App loads — Dashboard's template renders for the first time</strong><p>The template has <code>[chartData]="myChartData()"</code>. Angular calls <code>myChartData()</code> during this render and records: <em>"Dashboard's template is watching myChartData."</em> Notify list is now <code>[ Dashboard's template ]</code>.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div class="sig-trace-step sig-step-blue">
              <div class="sig-trace-num sig-num-blue">2</div>
              <div class="sig-trace-body"><strong>User clicks "Load Data" in Dashboard</strong><p>The click handler fires. An HTTP request goes out. Angular is not scanning anything — it is waiting.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div class="sig-trace-step sig-step-blue">
              <div class="sig-trace-num sig-num-blue">3</div>
              <div class="sig-trace-body"><strong>HTTP response arrives, <code>subscribe</code> callback runs</strong><p>The chart data is ready. The critical line is about to execute.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div class="sig-trace-step sig-step-green">
              <div class="sig-trace-num sig-num-green">4</div>
              <div class="sig-trace-body"><strong><code>this.myChartData.set(response)</code> — the signal checks its notify list</strong><p>The signal does not silently swap its value. It says: <em>"I changed — who is on my list?"</em> It finds: <code>[ Dashboard's template ]</code>. One lookup. No tree scan.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div class="sig-trace-step sig-step-green">
              <div class="sig-trace-num sig-num-green">5</div>
              <div class="sig-trace-body"><strong>Dashboard re-renders — passes new value to Chart via @Input</strong><p>Angular re-renders Dashboard's template. That template has <code>[chartData]="myChartData()"</code> — so Chart receives the updated value as a normal <code>@Input</code>. Chart re-renders because its <code>@Input</code> changed. Chart never subscribed to <code>myChartData</code> directly.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div class="sig-trace-step sig-step-red">
              <div class="sig-trace-num sig-num-red">6</div>
              <div class="sig-trace-body"><strong>AppRoot, Navbar, Table, ChartLegend, ChartTooltip, TableHeader, TableRow — never visited</strong><p>None of these templates ever called <code>myChartData()</code>. They are not on the notify list. Angular does not touch them — not even to check a diff.</p></div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="sig-tree-wrap mb-md">
            <div class="sig-tree-header">
              <span class="sig-tree-title">The same 9-component tree — signal update</span>
              <span class="sig-tree-badge">1 watcher &nbsp;·&nbsp; 1 @Input update &nbsp;·&nbsp; 7 never visited</span>
            </div>
            <div class="sig-walk">
              <div class="sig-node sig-node-idle">AppRoot <span class="sig-tag sig-tag-grey">not on notify list — never visited</span></div>
              <div class="nsw-children">
                <div class="sig-node sig-node-idle">Navbar <span class="sig-tag sig-tag-grey">not on notify list — never visited</span></div>
                <div class="sig-node sig-node-target">Dashboard <span class="sig-tag sig-tag-green">⚡ template called myChartData() — on notify list, re-renders</span></div>
                <div class="nsw-children">
                  <div class="sig-node sig-node-input">Chart 📈 <span class="sig-tag sig-tag-blue">@Input changed — re-renders (not a watcher)</span></div>
                  <div class="nsw-children">
                    <div class="sig-node sig-node-idle">ChartLegend <span class="sig-tag sig-tag-grey">not on notify list — never visited</span></div>
                    <div class="sig-node sig-node-idle">ChartTooltip <span class="sig-tag sig-tag-grey">not on notify list — never visited</span></div>
                  </div>
                  <div class="sig-node sig-node-idle">Table <span class="sig-tag sig-tag-grey">not on notify list — never visited</span></div>
                  <div class="nsw-children">
                    <div class="sig-node sig-node-idle">TableHeader <span class="sig-tag sig-tag-grey">not on notify list — never visited</span></div>
                    <div class="sig-node sig-node-idle">TableRow <span class="sig-tag sig-tag-grey">not on notify list — never visited</span></div>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="g2 mb-md">
            <div class="sticker sticker-red">
              <header class="sticker-title">📣 Zone.js — Shouts at Everyone</header>
              <div class="explain-rows">
                <div class="explain-row"><span class="explain-num" style="background:var(--red-pale);color:var(--red);">✗</span><span>An async event happened — Angular scans the whole tree</span></div>
                <div class="explain-row"><span class="explain-num" style="background:var(--red-pale);color:var(--red);">✗</span><span>All 9 components checked every single time</span></div>
                <div class="explain-row"><span class="explain-num" style="background:var(--red-pale);color:var(--red);">✗</span><span>Cost grows as your tree gets bigger</span></div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="sticker sticker-green">
              <header class="sticker-title">🤫 Signals — Whisper to the Right Component</header>
              <div class="explain-rows">
                <div class="explain-row"><span class="explain-num explain-num-green">✓</span><span>The signal knows its own notify list — no scan needed</span></div>
                <div class="explain-row"><span class="explain-num explain-num-green">✓</span><span>1 watcher (Dashboard) + 1 @Input update (Chart)</span></div>
                <div class="explain-row"><span class="explain-num explain-num-green">✓</span><span>Cost = number of watchers, not tree size</span></div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="callout callout-blue">
            <header class="callout-title callout-title-blue">💡 What if Table also reads myChartData()?</header>
            <p>If Table's template also binds <code>[tableData]="myChartData()"</code>, Angular registers Table as a second watcher. The notify list becomes <code>[ Dashboard's template, Table's template ]</code>. When <code>myChartData.set(response)</code> runs, Angular re-renders <strong>both</strong> — still nothing else. Two precise updates. The other 7 components are never visited.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="callout callout-yellow">
            <header class="callout-title callout-title-yellow">🤔 What does Chart actually receive? And does calling chartData() inside Chart register it as a subscriber?</header>
            <p style="margin-bottom:.7rem;">When Dashboard's template runs <code>[chartData]="myChartData()"</code>, Angular calls <code>myChartData()</code> right there — reads the current value, say <code>ChartData[]</code> — and hands that <strong>plain array</strong> to Chart as an <code>@Input</code>. The signal itself never travels to Chart. Chart's <code>chartData</code> property is just a regular array, nothing more.</p>
            <p style="margin-bottom:.7rem;">So if you call <code>this.chartData</code> (or loop over it) inside Chart's class or template — you are working with a plain array. There is no signal to subscribe to. <strong>Chart has no idea the data originally came from a signal.</strong></p>
            <p>The reactive wire ends at Dashboard's template. Think of it like a water pipe: the signal is the tap, Dashboard's template is where the tap is open, and Chart's <code>@Input</code> just receives a cup of water — not a connection to the tap.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="callout callout-blue">
            <header class="callout-title callout-title-blue">🤔 Wait — what is @Input and why is it here?</header>
            <p style="margin-bottom:.7rem;"><code>@Input()</code> is an Angular decorator that marks a property as <strong>settable from the parent's template</strong>. Without it, Dashboard's template cannot assign a value to Chart's property.</p>
            <p style="margin-bottom:.7rem;">When Dashboard writes <code>[chartData]="myChartData()"</code>, Angular internally does this:</p>
            <div class="code-wrap" style="margin-bottom:.7rem;">
              <div class="code-bar"><span class="code-filename">what Angular does behind the scenes</span></div>
              <div class="code-body" style="font-size:.78rem;">chartComponentInstance.chartData = myChartData();&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// plain assignment</span></div>
            </div>
            <p>That's it. <code>@Input()</code> is the permission slip that lets a parent set a child's property. There is no signal involved on Chart's side — it just receives a plain value every time Dashboard re-renders.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mental-section">
          <header class="route-section-title">Can Navbar Subscribe to Dashboard's Signal?</header>
          <p class="sec-lead" style="margin-bottom:1rem;"><code>myChartData = signal&lt;ChartData[]&gt;([])</code> is a class property on <code>DashboardComponent</code>. It has zero visibility outside that class — same as any other private property.</p>

          <div class="sig-tree-wrap mb-md">
            <div class="sig-tree-header">
              <span class="sig-tree-title">Navbar and Dashboard are siblings — no shared signal scope</span>
            </div>
            <div class="sig-walk">
              <div class="sig-node sig-node-idle">AppRoot <span class="sig-tag sig-tag-grey">no access to Dashboard's internals</span></div>
              <div class="nsw-children">
                <div class="sig-node sig-node-idle" style="border-color:var(--red);color:var(--red);">Navbar <span class="sig-tag" style="background:var(--red-pale);color:var(--red);border:1px solid var(--red);">✗ cannot access myChartData — it's a sibling, not a child</span></div>
                <div class="sig-node sig-node-target">Dashboard <span class="sig-tag sig-tag-green">myChartData = signal&lt;ChartData[]&gt;([]) lives here — private to this class</span></div>
                <div class="nsw-children">
                  <div class="sig-node sig-node-input">Chart 📈 <span class="sig-tag sig-tag-blue">can receive value via @Input — but only because Dashboard passes it down</span></div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="g2 mb-md">
            <div class="sticker sticker-top-green">
              <header class="sticker-title">Option 1 — Signal in the Component</header>
              <p class="card-body" style="margin-bottom:.6rem;">The signal lives in <code>DashboardComponent</code>. Only Dashboard's template is the watcher. Children like Chart can receive the value as an <code>@Input</code>, but siblings like Navbar cannot subscribe at all.</p>
              <div class="chip-row"><span class="chip chip-green">✓ Simple</span><span class="chip chip-green">✓ No service needed</span><span class="chip chip-red">✗ Siblings can't subscribe</span></div>
            </div>
            <div class="sticker sticker-top-green">
              <header class="sticker-title">Option 2 — Signal in a Service</header>
              <p class="card-body" style="margin-bottom:.6rem;">Lift the signal into a shared <code>@Injectable</code> service. Now any component that injects the service can call <code>service.myChartData()</code> in its template — Navbar, Dashboard, Chart, or anyone else — and each becomes a watcher on the same notify list.</p>
              <div class="chip-row"><span class="chip chip-green">✓ Cross-component sharing</span><span class="chip chip-green">✓ Siblings can subscribe</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="callout callout-yellow">
            <header class="callout-title callout-title-yellow">⚠️ The scope rule in one sentence</header>
            <p>A signal defined in a component class is scoped to that component. To share a signal between components that are not in a parent→child relationship, move it into a Service.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="callout callout-green">
          <header class="callout-title callout-title-green">🔑 The Key Rule</header>
          <p>Angular only tracks signal reads that happen in a <strong>reactive context</strong> — inside a component template, a <code>computed()</code>, or an <code>effect()</code>. Reading a signal in plain TypeScript code (not in a template or effect) won't trigger any re-render.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ════ 03 SIGNAL VS PROPERTY ════ -->
      <section data-sid="signal-vs-prop" class="section sec-panel">
        <p class="eyebrow">03 · Signal vs Property</p>
        <h2 class="sec-title">Why Not Just Use a <span>Normal Variable</span>?</h2>
        <p class="sec-lead">
          This is the question every developer asks first. The answer reveals why Signals exist at all.
          <strong>JavaScript has no native way to watch a variable for changes</strong> — Angular had to invent one.
        </p>

        <div class="mental-section">
          <header class="route-section-title">The Sticky Note vs the Group Chat</header>
          <div class="g2 mb-md">
            <div class="sticker sticker-red">
              <header class="sticker-title">📝 Regular Property = Sticky Note on Your Desk</header>
              <p class="card-body">You write a number on a sticky note. Your colleague has to physically walk over and check your desk every few minutes to see if the number changed. <strong>That's Zone.js</strong> — Angular walking your entire component tree after every event, reading every variable manually.</p>
              <p class="card-body" style="margin-top:.5rem;"><strong>Problem:</strong> it walks even when nothing changed. And JavaScript has no <code>onChange</code> event for plain properties.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="sticker sticker-green">
              <header class="sticker-title">💬 Signal = A Group Chat Message</header>
              <p class="card-body">You post the new number in a group chat. <strong>Only the people who joined that chat get notified.</strong> Nobody else is disturbed. The notification is instant, and nobody has to keep checking.</p>
              <p class="card-body" style="margin-top:.5rem;"><strong>In Angular terms:</strong> every template that calls <code>count()</code> has "joined the chat." When <code>count.set()</code> is called, only those templates re-render.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mental-section">
          <header class="subsec-label">Three Options Side by Side</header>
          <div class="g3">
            <div class="sticker sticker-left-red">
              <header class="sticker-title">Regular Property</header>
              <div class="code-wrap mt-sm">
                <div class="code-body code-body-sm">
                  count = <span class="vl">0</span>;<br/>
                  count = <span class="vl">5</span>; <span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// silent</span>
                </div>
              </div>
              <p class="card-body"><strong class="c-ink">Template:</strong> <code>&#123;&#123; count &#125;&#125;</code><br/><strong class="c-ink">Angular detects change:</strong> Zone.js polls everything<br/><strong class="c-ink">Good for:</strong> static display values only</p>
            </div>
            <div class="sticker sticker-left-green">
              <header class="sticker-title">Signal</header>
              <div class="code-wrap mt-sm">
                <div class="code-body code-body-sm">
                  count = <span class="fn">signal</span>(<span class="vl">0</span>);<br/>
                  count.<span class="fn">set</span>(<span class="vl">5</span>); <span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// notifies</span>
                </div>
              </div>
              <p class="card-body"><strong class="c-ink">Template:</strong> <code>&#123;&#123; count() &#125;&#125;</code><br/><strong class="c-ink">Angular detects change:</strong> signal notifies Angular directly<br/><strong class="c-ink">Good for:</strong> all UI state</p>
            </div>
            
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="callout callout-yellow">
          <header class="callout-title callout-title-yellow">⚠️ The () Is Not Optional</header>
          <p>With a regular property you write <code>&#123;&#123; count &#125;&#125;</code>. With a signal you write <code>&#123;&#123; count() &#125;&#125;</code>. That call is how Angular registers your template as a "watcher." Forgetting the <code>()</code> renders the signal function object itself — you'll see something like <code>[Function]</code> on screen.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ════ 04 CREATING SIGNALS ════ -->
      <section data-sid="creating-signals" class="section sec-panel">
        <p class="eyebrow">04 · Creating Signals</p>
        <h2 class="sec-title">The Whiteboard <span>Number</span></h2>
        <p class="sec-lead">
          Think of a writable signal as a whiteboard with a number written on it.
          Anyone can look at it. You control who can change it.
          There are exactly 3 ways to interact with it.
        </p>

        <div class="mental-section">
          <header class="route-section-title">The 3 Operations — With the Whiteboard Analogy</header>
          <div class="sol-steps">
            <div class="sol-step">
              <div class="sol-num sol-num-green">1</div>
              <div>
                <p class="card-title"><code>signal(initialValue)</code> — Hang the whiteboard</p>
                <p class="card-body">Creates a new writable signal with a starting value. TypeScript infers the type — you don't need to declare it.</p>
                <div class="code-wrap">
                  <div class="code-bar"><div class="code-dots"><div class="code-dot" style="background:#ff5f57;"></div><div class="code-dot" style="background:#febc2e;"></div><div class="code-dot" style="background:#28c840;"></div></div><span class="code-filename">component.ts</span></div>
                  <div class="code-body code-body-sm">
                    <span class="ky">const</span> count = <span class="fn">signal</span>(<span class="vl">0</span>);&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// number</span><br/>
                    <span class="ky">const</span> name&nbsp; = <span class="fn">signal</span>(<span class="st">'Alice'</span>);&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// string</span><br/>
                    <span class="ky">const</span> items = <span class="fn">signal</span>([<span class="st">'a'</span>, <span class="st">'b'</span>]);&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// string[]</span><br/>
                    <span class="ky">const</span> user&nbsp; = <span class="fn">signal</span>&lt;User | <span class="ky">null</span>&gt;(<span class="ky">null</span>);&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// explicit generic</span>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="sol-step">
              <div class="sol-num sol-num-blue">2</div>
              <div>
                <p class="card-title"><code>.set(newValue)</code> — Erase and rewrite the number</p>
                <p class="card-body">Replaces the current value with a completely new one. Use when you know exactly what the new value should be.</p>
                <div class="code-wrap">
                  <div class="code-bar"><div class="code-dots"><div class="code-dot" style="background:#ff5f57;"></div><div class="code-dot" style="background:#febc2e;"></div><div class="code-dot" style="background:#28c840;"></div></div><span class="code-filename">component.ts</span></div>
                  <div class="code-body code-body-sm">
                    count.<span class="fn">set</span>(<span class="vl">10</span>);&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// was 0, now 10</span><br/>
                    name.<span class="fn">set</span>(<span class="st">'Bob'</span>);&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// was 'Alice', now 'Bob'</span><br/>
                    user.<span class="fn">set</span>(&#123; id: <span class="vl">1</span>, name: <span class="st">'Bob'</span> &#125;);
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="sol-step">
              <div class="sol-num sol-num-orange">3</div>
              <div>
                <p class="card-title"><code>.update(fn)</code> — Change based on what's already written</p>
                <p class="card-body">Computes the new value from the current one. Equivalent to <code>count = count + 1</code> but for signals. Use when the new value depends on the old one.</p>
                <div class="code-wrap">
                  <div class="code-bar"><div class="code-dots"><div class="code-dot" style="background:#ff5f57;"></div><div class="code-dot" style="background:#febc2e;"></div><div class="code-dot" style="background:#28c840;"></div></div><span class="code-filename">component.ts</span></div>
                  <div class="code-body code-body-sm">
                    count.<span class="fn">update</span>(v =&gt; v + <span class="vl">1</span>);&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// increment</span><br/>
                    count.<span class="fn">update</span>(v =&gt; v * <span class="vl">2</span>);&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// double it</span><br/>
                    items.<span class="fn">update</span>(arr =&gt; [...arr, <span class="st">'c'</span>]);&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// add to array</span>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mental-section">
          <header class="subsec-label">Real Component Example</header>
          <div class="code-wrap">
            <div class="code-bar"><div class="code-dots"><div class="code-dot" style="background:#ff5f57;"></div><div class="code-dot" style="background:#febc2e;"></div><div class="code-dot" style="background:#28c840;"></div></div><span class="code-filename">counter.component.ts</span></div>
            <div class="code-body">
              <span class="ky">@Component</span>(&#123;<br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hi">selector</span>: <span class="st">'app-counter'</span>,<br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hi">template</span>: <span class="st">`<br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;p&gt;Count: &#123;&#123; count() &#125;&#125;&lt;/p&gt;<br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;button (click)="increment()"&gt;+1&lt;/button&gt;<br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;button (click)="reset()"&gt;Reset&lt;/button&gt;<br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;`</span>,<br/>
              &#125;)<br/>
              <span class="ky">export class</span> <span class="fn">CounterComponent</span> &#123;<br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;count = <span class="fn">signal</span>(<span class="vl">0</span>);<br/><br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="fn">increment</span>() &#123; <span class="ky">this</span>.count.<span class="fn">update</span>(v =&gt; v + <span class="vl">1</span>); &#125;<br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="fn">reset</span>()&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;() &#123; <span class="ky">this</span>.count.<span class="fn">set</span>(<span class="vl">0</span>); &#125;<br/>
              &#125;
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ════ 05 COMPUTED ════ -->
      <section data-sid="computed" class="section sec-panel">
        <p class="eyebrow">05 · computed()</p>
        <h2 class="sec-title">The Spreadsheet <span>Formula Cell</span></h2>
        <p class="sec-lead">
          If you've used Excel, you know that a formula cell like <code>=A1*2</code> always shows double whatever A1 is.
          You never manually update it — it stays correct automatically.
          <strong><code>computed()</code> is exactly that — a derived value that always stays in sync.</strong>
        </p>

        <div class="mental-section">
          <header class="route-section-title">How computed() Works</header>
          <div class="sticker sticker-top-green mb-md">
            <header class="sticker-title">📊 Excel Analogy</header>
            <div class="explain-rows">
              <div class="explain-row"><span class="explain-num explain-num-green">A1</span><span>Cell A1 = <code>const count = signal(5)</code> — your raw data</span></div>
              <div class="explain-row"><span class="explain-num explain-num-green">B1</span><span>Cell B1 = <code>=A1*2</code> → <code>const doubled = computed(() =&gt; count() * 2)</code></span></div>
              <div class="explain-row"><span class="explain-num explain-num-green">→</span><span>Change A1 to 7 → B1 automatically shows 14. <strong class="c-ink">You never touch B1.</strong></span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mental-section">
          <header class="subsec-label">computed() in Code</header>
          <div class="code-wrap mb-md">
            <div class="code-bar"><div class="code-dots"><div class="code-dot" style="background:#ff5f57;"></div><div class="code-dot" style="background:#febc2e;"></div><div class="code-dot" style="background:#28c840;"></div></div><span class="code-filename">component.ts</span></div>
            <div class="code-body">
              <span class="ky">const</span> count   = <span class="fn">signal</span>(<span class="vl">5</span>);<br/>
              <span class="ky">const</span> doubled = <span class="fn">computed</span>(() =&gt; count() * <span class="vl">2</span>);<br/><br/>
              doubled();&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// → 10</span><br/>
              count.<span class="fn">set</span>(<span class="vl">7</span>);<br/>
              doubled();&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// → 14  ← automatically correct</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mental-section">
          <header class="subsec-label">3 Rules About computed()</header>
          <div class="g3">
            <div class="sticker sticker-left-green">
              <header class="sticker-title">😴 Lazy</header>
              <p class="card-body">The formula only runs when you actually read it. If nothing reads <code>doubled()</code>, Angular never calculates it. <strong>No wasted work.</strong></p>
            </div>
            <div class="sticker sticker-left-blue">
              <header class="sticker-title">💾 Memoized</header>
              <p class="card-body">Once calculated, the result is cached. Reading <code>doubled()</code> 100 times in a row without <code>count</code> changing only runs the formula <strong>once</strong>.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="sticker sticker-left-red">
              <header class="sticker-title">🔒 Read-Only</header>
              <p class="card-body"><code>doubled.set(99)</code> is a <strong>compile error</strong>. You can only change the source signal (<code>count</code>). Computed values are derived, never set directly.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mental-section">
          <header class="subsec-label">Real-World Example — Shopping Cart</header>
          <div class="code-wrap">
            <div class="code-bar"><div class="code-dots"><div class="code-dot" style="background:#ff5f57;"></div><div class="code-dot" style="background:#febc2e;"></div><div class="code-dot" style="background:#28c840;"></div></div><span class="code-filename">cart.component.ts</span></div>
            <div class="code-body">
              items = <span class="fn">signal</span>([<br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;&#123; name: <span class="st">'Book'</span>,   price: <span class="vl">12</span> &#125;,<br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;&#123; name: <span class="st">'Pen'</span>,    price: <span class="vl">3</span>  &#125;,<br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;&#123; name: <span class="st">'Laptop'</span>, price: <span class="vl">999</span> &#125;,<br/>
              ]);<br/><br/>
              <span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// These auto-update whenever items changes:</span><br/>
              total&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; = <span class="fn">computed</span>(() =&gt; <span class="ky">this</span>.items().<span class="fn">reduce</span>((s, i) =&gt; s + i.price, <span class="vl">0</span>));<br/>
              itemCount = <span class="fn">computed</span>(() =&gt; <span class="ky">this</span>.items().length);<br/>
              hasItems  = <span class="fn">computed</span>(() =&gt; <span class="ky">this</span>.items().length &gt; <span class="vl">0</span>);<br/><br/>
              <span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// Add an item — total, itemCount, hasItems all update automatically</span><br/>
              <span class="fn">addItem</span>(item: Item) &#123;<br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="ky">this</span>.items.<span class="fn">update</span>(arr =&gt; [...arr, item]);<br/>
              &#125;
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ════ 06 EFFECTS ════ -->
      <section data-sid="effects" class="section sec-panel">
        <p class="eyebrow">06 · effect()</p>
        <h2 class="sec-title">Push <span>Notifications</span> for Your Code</h2>
        <p class="sec-lead">
          <code>computed()</code> derives a new value. But sometimes you need to <em>do something</em>
          when state changes — save to localStorage, log an event, call a non-Angular API.
          <strong><code>effect()</code> is a callback that runs whenever its signals change.</strong>
        </p>

        <div class="mental-section">
          <header class="route-section-title">The Push Notification Analogy</header>
          <div class="g2 mb-md">
            <div class="sticker sticker-red">
              <header class="sticker-title">📧 Old Way — Manual Polling</header>
              <p class="card-body">Every 30 seconds your phone opens your email app and checks for new messages. Even at 3am. Even when there's nothing new. <strong>That's Zone.js change detection on intervals.</strong></p>
            </div>
            <div class="sticker sticker-green">
              <header class="sticker-title">🔔 effect() Way — Push Notification</header>
              <p class="card-body">The server sends a ping the moment a new email arrives. Your phone only wakes up when there's something to do. <strong>That's effect() — runs only when a tracked signal changes.</strong></p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mental-section">
          <header class="subsec-label">effect() in Code</header>
          <div class="code-wrap mb-md">
            <div class="code-bar"><div class="code-dots"><div class="code-dot" style="background:#ff5f57;"></div><div class="code-dot" style="background:#febc2e;"></div><div class="code-dot" style="background:#28c840;"></div></div><span class="code-filename">component.ts</span></div>
            <div class="code-body">
              theme = <span class="fn">signal</span>(<span class="st">'light'</span>);<br/>
              fontSize = <span class="fn">signal</span>(<span class="vl">16</span>);<br/><br/>
              <span class="ky">constructor</span>() &#123;<br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// Runs immediately, then again whenever theme OR fontSize changes</span><br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="fn">effect</span>(() =&gt; &#123;<br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;document.body.className = <span class="ky">this</span>.<span class="fn">theme</span>();&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// tracked</span><br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;document.body.style.fontSize = <span class="ky">this</span>.<span class="fn">fontSize</span>() + <span class="st">'px'</span>;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// tracked</span><br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;&#125;);<br/>
              &#125;
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mental-section">
          <header class="subsec-label">3 Rules About effect()</header>
          <div class="sol-steps">
            <div class="sol-step">
              <div class="sol-num sol-num-green">1</div>
              <div>
                <p class="card-title">Every signal read inside the callback becomes a dependency</p>
                <p class="card-body">Angular automatically tracks which signals you read inside the <code>effect()</code> callback. When any of them change, the callback runs again. <strong>No manual subscription list needed.</strong></p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="sol-step">
              <div class="sol-num sol-num-blue">2</div>
              <div>
                <p class="card-title">It runs once immediately, then on each change</p>
                <p class="card-body">When you create an <code>effect()</code>, it runs once right away (to establish dependencies). After that, it re-runs whenever a tracked signal changes. <strong>No need to call it manually the first time.</strong></p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="sol-step">
              <div class="sol-num sol-num-orange">3</div>
              <div>
                <p class="card-title">Don't use effect() to update signals — use computed() instead</p>
                <p class="card-body">If you find yourself writing <code>effect(() =&gt; &#123; otherSignal.set(thisSignal()) &#125;)</code>, that's a sign you should use <code>computed()</code>. <strong>Effects are for side effects on non-reactive APIs</strong> (DOM, localStorage, third-party libraries) — not for deriving state.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ════ 07 READONLY ════ -->
      <section data-sid="readonly" class="section sec-panel">
        <p class="eyebrow">07 · asReadonly()</p>
        <h2 class="sec-title">Your Bank Balance — <span>Visible but Protected</span></h2>
        <p class="sec-lead">
          You can check your bank balance any time you want. But you can't walk into the bank
          and type any number you feel like. <strong><code>asReadonly()</code> exposes a signal's value
          without giving write access.</strong>
        </p>

        <div class="mental-section">
          <header class="route-section-title">The Problem It Solves</header>
          <div class="callout callout-red-left mb-md">
            <header class="callout-title callout-title-red">Without asReadonly() — any consumer can break your state</header>
            <p>If you expose a writable signal from a service, any component that injects the service can call <code>count.set(-999)</code>. <strong>You have zero control over who modifies your state.</strong></p>
          </div>
          <div class="callout callout-green mb-md">
            <header class="callout-title callout-title-green">With asReadonly() — consumers can see but not touch</header>
            <p>You keep the writable signal private. You expose a readonly version. Components can read <code>count()</code> freely. Any attempt to call <code>count.set()</code> is a <strong>TypeScript compile error.</strong></p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mental-section">
          <header class="subsec-label">The Pattern — Service with Encapsulated State</header>
          <div class="code-wrap">
            <div class="code-bar"><div class="code-dots"><div class="code-dot" style="background:#ff5f57;"></div><div class="code-dot" style="background:#febc2e;"></div><div class="code-dot" style="background:#28c840;"></div></div><span class="code-filename">counter.service.ts</span></div>
            <div class="code-body">
              <span class="ky">@Injectable</span>(&#123; <span class="hi">providedIn</span>: <span class="st">'root'</span> &#125;)<br/>
              <span class="ky">export class</span> <span class="fn">CounterService</span> &#123;<br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// ① Private writable — only THIS service can change it</span><br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="ky">private readonly</span> _count = <span class="fn">signal</span>(<span class="vl">0</span>);<br/><br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// ② Public readonly — anyone can READ, nobody can SET</span><br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="ky">readonly</span> count = <span class="ky">this</span>._count.<span class="fn">asReadonly</span>();<br/><br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// ③ Controlled mutations through methods only</span><br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="fn">increment</span>() &#123; <span class="ky">this</span>._count.<span class="fn">update</span>(v =&gt; v + <span class="vl">1</span>); &#125;<br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="fn">reset</span>()&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; &#123; <span class="ky">this</span>._count.<span class="fn">set</span>(<span class="vl">0</span>); &#125;<br/>
              &#125;<br/><br/>
              <span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// In any component:</span><br/>
              counter = <span class="fn">inject</span>(<span class="fn">CounterService</span>);<br/>
              <span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// counter.count()         ✅ read — fine</span><br/>
              <span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// counter.count.set(99)   ❌ TypeScript error — readonly</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ════ 08 UNTRACKED ════ -->
      <section data-sid="untracked" class="section sec-panel">
        <p class="eyebrow">08 · untracked()</p>
        <h2 class="sec-title">Peeking Without <span>Subscribing</span></h2>
        <p class="sec-lead">
          Normally, reading a signal inside a <code>computed()</code> or <code>effect()</code>
          registers it as a dependency — the effect re-runs whenever that signal changes.
          <strong><code>untracked()</code> lets you read the current value without creating that subscription.</strong>
        </p>

        <div class="mental-section">
          <header class="route-section-title">The Stadium Analogy</header>
          <div class="sticker sticker-top-blue mb-md">
            <header class="sticker-title">🏟️ The Difference Between a Fan and a Passerby</header>
            <p class="card-body">
              You're at a stadium, watching the <strong>home team score</strong> (you're a registered fan — they text you updates).
              You glance at the away team score on the board — you look at it, but you don't want the stadium
              to start texting you every time the away team scores. You're <strong>reading without subscribing.</strong>
            </p>
            <p class="card-body" style="margin-top:.5rem;">
              <code>untracked(awayScore)</code> = peeking at the away score as a passerby. Angular won't include it in your subscriptions.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mental-section">
          <header class="subsec-label">untracked() in Code</header>
          <div class="g2 mb-md">
            <div class="ba-code-panel ba-code-bad">
              <div class="ba-code-head"><header>❌ Counter re-runs on BOTH signal changes</header></div>
              <div class="code-body" style="font-size:.7rem;border-radius:0;">
                <span class="fn">effect</span>(() =&gt; &#123;<br/>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// Both are tracked — effect fires if EITHER changes</span><br/>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;console.<span class="fn">log</span>(<span class="ky">this</span>.<span class="fn">currentUser</span>());<br/>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;console.<span class="fn">log</span>(<span class="ky">this</span>.<span class="fn">counter</span>());&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--rd);">// unintended dependency</span><br/>
                &#125;);
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="ba-code-panel ba-code-good">
              <div class="ba-code-head"><header>✅ Only fires when currentUser changes</header></div>
              <div class="code-body" style="font-size:.7rem;border-radius:0;">
                <span class="fn">effect</span>(() =&gt; &#123;<br/>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// Only currentUser is tracked</span><br/>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;console.<span class="fn">log</span>(<span class="ky">this</span>.<span class="fn">currentUser</span>());<br/>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;console.<span class="fn">log</span>(<span class="fn">untracked</span>(<span class="ky">this</span>.counter)); <span style="color:var(--ok);">// ✅ peek</span><br/>
                &#125;);
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="callout callout-blue">
          <header class="callout-title callout-title-blue">When to use untracked()</header>
          <p>Use it when a reactive function (effect or computed) needs to <strong>read a signal for context</strong> without being re-triggered by it. Common case: logging the value of a secondary signal alongside the primary one, without making the secondary a dependency.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ════ 09 GOTCHAS ════ -->
      <section data-sid="gotchas" class="section sec-panel">
        <p class="eyebrow">09 · The Rules</p>
        <h2 class="sec-title">3 Things That Will <span>Trip You Up</span></h2>
        <p class="sec-lead">
          Signals are simple — but there are three specific patterns that surprise developers coming from Zone.js.
          Know these before you hit them in production.
        </p>

        <div class="sol-steps">
          <div class="sol-step sol-step-err">
            <div class="sol-num sol-num-err">1</div>
            <div>
              <header class="sticker-title">async + signals = you lose the reactive context after <code>await</code></header>
              <p class="card-body">The reactive tracking system only works for synchronous code. <strong>Any signal read that happens after an <code>await</code> is not tracked.</strong> Angular can't create a dependency for code that runs later in a microtask queue.</p>
              <div class="g2">
                <div class="ba-code-panel ba-code-bad">
                  <div class="ba-code-head"><header>❌ theme() not tracked — context lost after await</header></div>
                  <div class="code-body" style="font-size:.68rem;border-radius:0;">
                    <span class="fn">effect</span>(<span class="ky">async</span> () =&gt; &#123;<br/>
                    &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="ky">const</span> data = <span class="ky">await</span> <span class="fn">fetchData</span>();<br/>
                    &nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--red);">theme();</span>&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// 💥 not tracked</span><br/>
                    &#125;);
                  </div>
                </div>
                <div class="ba-code-panel ba-code-good">
                  <div class="ba-code-head"><header>✅ Read signals BEFORE the await</header></div>
                  <div class="code-body" style="font-size:.68rem;border-radius:0;">
                    <span class="fn">effect</span>(<span class="ky">async</span> () =&gt; &#123;<br/>
                    &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="ky">const</span> t = <span class="fn">theme</span>();&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// ✅ tracked</span><br/>
                    &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="ky">const</span> data = <span class="ky">await</span> <span class="fn">fetchData</span>();<br/>
                    &#125;);
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="sol-step">
            <div class="sol-num sol-num-orange">2</div>
            <div>
              <p class="card-title">computed() is lazy — it doesn't run until you read it</p>
              <p class="card-body">Don't rely on a computed signal "running in the background" to keep things up to date. <strong>It only recalculates when something reads it.</strong> If nothing reads it, the derivation never runs. This is a feature (no wasted work) but can surprise you if you expect it to run eagerly.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="sol-step">
            <div class="sol-num sol-num-blue">3</div>
            <div>
              <p class="card-title">Arrays and objects: <code>.set()</code> with a new reference, not a mutation</p>
              <p class="card-body">Signals use <code>Object.is()</code> to check if the value changed. If you mutate an array directly (<code>items().push('x')</code>), the reference stays the same — Angular sees "no change" and doesn't re-render. <strong>Always create a new array/object with the change applied.</strong></p>
              <div class="g2">
                <div class="ba-code-panel ba-code-bad">
                  <div class="ba-code-head"><header>❌ Mutates in place — no re-render</header></div>
                  <div class="code-body" style="font-size:.68rem;border-radius:0;">
                    <span class="rd">items().<span class="fn">push</span>(<span class="st">'new'</span>);</span>&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// same ref</span>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <div class="ba-code-panel ba-code-good">
                  <div class="ba-code-head"><header>✅ New array reference — Angular sees the change</header></div>
                  <div class="code-body" style="font-size:.68rem;border-radius:0;">
                    items.<span class="fn">update</span>(arr =&gt; [...arr, <span class="st">'new'</span>]);
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ════ 10 RXJS ════ -->
      <section data-sid="rxjs" class="section sec-panel">
        <p class="eyebrow">10 · Signals vs RxJS</p>
        <h2 class="sec-title">Which One Do <span>I Use?</span></h2>
        <p class="sec-lead">
          Signals and RxJS are not enemies — they solve different problems.
          The confusion is that both deal with "values that change over time."
          <strong>The difference is in how they change and what you want to do with them.</strong>
        </p>

        <div class="mental-section">
          <header class="route-section-title">The Simple Rule</header>
          <div class="g2 mb-md">
            <div class="sticker sticker-left-green">
              <div class="sticker-emoji">⚡</div>
              <header class="sticker-title">Use Signals for...</header>
              <div class="explain-rows">
                <div class="explain-row"><span class="explain-num explain-num-green">→</span><span><strong class="c-ink">UI state</strong> — the selected tab, a counter, form values, loading state</span></div>
                <div class="explain-row"><span class="explain-num explain-num-green">→</span><span><strong class="c-ink">Derived state</strong> — total price, filtered list, formatted name</span></div>
                <div class="explain-row"><span class="explain-num explain-num-green">→</span><span><strong class="c-ink">Shared app state</strong> — replacing a simple <code>BehaviorSubject</code> in a service</span></div>
                <div class="explain-row"><span class="explain-num explain-num-green">→</span><span><strong class="c-ink">Component inputs</strong> — <code>@Input()</code> is becoming a signal (<code>input()</code>)</span></div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="sticker sticker-left-blue">
              <div class="sticker-emoji">🌊</div>
              <header class="sticker-title">Use RxJS for...</header>
              <div class="explain-rows">
                <div class="explain-row"><span class="explain-num explain-num-blue">→</span><span><strong class="c-ink">HTTP calls</strong> — <code>HttpClient</code> returns Observables; keep using them</span></div>
                <div class="explain-row"><span class="explain-num explain-num-blue">→</span><span><strong class="c-ink">Event streams</strong> — user typing, WebSocket messages, scroll events</span></div>
                <div class="explain-row"><span class="explain-num explain-num-blue">→</span><span><strong class="c-ink">Complex async sequences</strong> — <code>switchMap</code>, <code>debounceTime</code>, <code>combineLatest</code></span></div>
                <div class="explain-row"><span class="explain-num explain-num-blue">→</span><span><strong class="c-ink">Multicasting and buffering</strong> — <code>shareReplay</code>, <code>buffer</code></span></div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mental-section">
          <header class="subsec-label">Bridging the Two Worlds</header>
          <p class="card-body" style="margin-bottom:1rem;">Angular provides two functions to convert between Signals and Observables. <strong>You can freely mix both in the same app.</strong></p>
          <div class="code-wrap mb-md">
            <div class="code-bar"><div class="code-dots"><div class="code-dot" style="background:#ff5f57;"></div><div class="code-dot" style="background:#febc2e;"></div><div class="code-dot" style="background:#28c840;"></div></div><span class="code-filename">component.ts</span></div>
            <div class="code-body">
              <span class="ky">import</span> &#123; toSignal, toObservable &#125; <span class="ky">from</span> <span class="st">'@angular/core/rxjs-interop'</span>;<br/><br/>
              <span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// Observable → Signal (use in template without async pipe)</span><br/>
              <span class="ky">const</span> data$ = <span class="ky">this</span>.http.<span class="fn">get</span>&lt;User[]&gt;(<span class="st">'/api/users'</span>);<br/>
              <span class="ky">const</span> users = <span class="fn">toSignal</span>(data$, &#123; <span class="hi">initialValue</span>: [] &#125;);<br/>
              <span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// template: &#123;&#123; users() &#125;&#125; — no async pipe needed</span><br/><br/>
              <span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// Signal → Observable (use RxJS operators on a signal's values)</span><br/>
              <span class="ky">const</span> search = <span class="fn">signal</span>(<span class="st">''</span>);<br/>
              <span class="ky">const</span> search$ = <span class="fn">toObservable</span>(search).<span class="fn">pipe</span>(<br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="fn">debounceTime</span>(<span class="vl">300</span>),<br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="fn">switchMap</span>(q =&gt; <span class="ky">this</span>.http.<span class="fn">get</span>(<span class="st">`/api/search?q=$&#123;q&#125;`</span>))<br/>
              );
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="callout callout-green">
          <header class="callout-title callout-title-green">🎯 The Bottom Line</header>
          <p>If it's <strong>state that your template shows</strong> → Signal. If it's <strong>a stream of events or async data from outside</strong> → RxJS. When you need both, use <code>toSignal()</code> to bring an Observable into the signal world.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

    </div><!-- /content-pane -->
  </div><!-- /app-shell -->

  <!-- ════ FINAL BANNER ════ -->
  <div class="final-banner">
    <h2>The Core Rule</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>A signal is a value with a built-in announcement system.</strong><br/>
      Read it with <code>signal()</code>. Change it with <code>.set()</code>. Derive from it with <code>computed()</code>. React to it with <code>effect()</code>. Everything else follows.
    </p>
  </div>

